The quintessential drive-thru frozen chocolate dessert made right in your kitchen with four simple ingredients. Freezing half the milk into ice cubes before blending is the secret to capturing that signature thick, spoonable, malt-shop consistency without diluting the rich cocoa flavor with watery ice cubes.

*Adapted from [Delish](https://www.delish.com/cooking/recipe-ideas/a36542097/copycat-wendys-frosty-recipe/).*

## Ingredients

- ⅔ cup cold whole milk, divided (⅓ cup frozen into cubes, ⅓ cup chilled)
- 8 oz frozen whipped topping (such as Cool Whip), kept fully frozen
- 2 tablespoons sweetened condensed milk
- 1 tablespoon unsweetened cocoa powder (natural or Dutch-processed)

## Instructions

1. **Freeze the Milk Base:** Pour ⅓ cup of the whole milk into standard ice cube tray wells (or a silicone cube tray). Freeze for 2 to 3 hours until completely solid.
2. **Combine Ingredients in Blender:** Pop the frozen milk cubes directly into your blender jar. Add the remaining ⅓ cup of cold liquid whole milk, the container of solid frozen whipped topping, the sweetened condensed milk, and the cocoa powder.
3. **Blend to Signature Texture:** Secure the blender lid. Pulse several times on medium speed to fracture the frozen milk cubes, then ramp up to high speed for 30 to 45 seconds. Use the tamper or stop and scrape down the sides once to ensure no cocoa powder clings to the walls. The mixture should become ultra-creamy, velvety, and dense enough to hold a spoon upright.
4. **Dish and Enjoy:** Divide the Frosty between two chilled glasses or sundae cups. Serve immediately with a long spoon and a wide straw.

## Notes

- **Extra Chocolate Boost:** For an even deeper chocolate profile, add an extra teaspoon of cocoa powder or drizzle the inside walls of the glasses with chocolate fudge syrup before filling.
- **Vanilla Frosty Variation:** Replace the cocoa powder with 1 teaspoon of pure vanilla extract for a creamy vanilla copycat frosty.
- **Consistency Tip:** If the blend is slightly too stiff for your blender motor to turn smoothly, drizzle in an extra tablespoon of cold milk at a time until the blade catches.
